{"conjecture":"|EJ| = |K P_y| (segments EJ and K P_y have equal length)","reason":"I found this by computing from the given coordinates, not by a full proof. EJ² ≈ 128.71 and KP_y² ≈ 128.71, so the two lengths agree to the precision given (≈11.345). The segments are not parallel, so this is not a parallelogram relation.

The structure behind it is as follows. In the right triangle K P_x P_y (right angle at P_x), K P_y is parallel to j, so the angle at K equals the angle between line FK and j. That is the angle at F in the right triangle F D K, which is right-angled at D because e ⟂ j. Hence K P_y = K P_x · FK / FD. Since h1 ⟂ j at V, we also have FP_x/FK = FV/FD. Combining these gives K P_y = FK·(FK − FP_x)/FD = FK²·(1 − FV/FD)/FD = FK²·VD/FD². Here FK²/FD² = 1 + DK²/FD², and DK = FD·(P_xV/FV), so it equals 1 + (P_xV/FV)², which is the P_x-dependent factor.

The point J is built from P_y by the chain H (the foot of P_y on j), then the parallel to GE, then the tangent g. This chain is a similarity from the P_x-dependent segment K P_y onto EJ. The construction therefore sends the P_x-dependence on both sides to the same scale factor. That gives EJ = K P_y for every position of P_x on h1. I verified this only numerically at the given P_x.","closes_goal":true}

