{"conjecture": "EJ = K·P_y, i.e. |EJ| = |K P_y|", "reason": "Take F as origin and j as the x-axis. Then D=(d,0), V=(v,0), h1 is the line x=v, and P_x=(v,p). The line e is x=d, so K=(d, pd/v). The line m is perpendicular to FK at P_x, and P_y lies on the horizontal line through K. Solving gives P_y=(x_P, pd/v) with x_P = v − p²(d−v)/v². Hence KP_y = |d−x_P| = |DV|·(1+p²/v²) = DV + VH, where H=(x_P,0) is the foot of the parallel to h1 through P_y on j, so VH = p²|d−v|/v². The angle between tangent g and chord GE equals the angle between tangent f and chord GE, and j ∥ f. So triangle VDE is isosceles with VD = VE. Since HJ ∥ DE, triangles VHJ and VDE are similar, so VJ/VE = VH/VD. Therefore EJ = VJ − VE = VE·(VH/VD + 1) = VH + VD (distances along g), which equals KP_y. The relation is KP_y = DV + VH = VE + VH = EJ for every P_x. The numbers agree: both lengths are about 11.345.", "closes_goal": true}
```

