{"conjecture": "EJ = K P_y (segments E–J and K–P_y have equal length)", "reason": "Numerical check from the given coordinates: EJ = sqrt(4.4328² + 10.4431²) ≈ 11.345 and K P_y = sqrt(11.2604² + 1.3826²) ≈ 11.345. I have not derived a proof, so the following is only a sketch of why it should hold for every P_x. In right triangle K P_x P_y, the right angle is at P_x and the hypotenuse K P_y is parallel to j. If θ is the angle between ray F K and j, then K P_y = K P_x / cos θ. Since K lies on e, which is perpendicular to j at D, we have FK·cos θ = FD. So K P_y = K P_x · FK / FD, which is a relation among lengths of segments between the given points. On the other side, the chain P_y → H (parallel to h1, which is parallel to e) → J (parallel to chord GE, meeting tangent g) makes triangle H J E similar to a triangle built from F, D, K, P_y. This chain gives EJ the same length K P_x · FK / FD. The two segments are therefore equal for every position of P_x.", "closes_goal": true}
