{"conjecture": "EJ = K·P_y, i.e. |EJ| = |K P_y| for every position of P_x.", "reason": "Because j is tangent to c at F, the tangents from V give VE = VF. Chord GE meets the tangent j at D, and the angles at D and E in triangle VDE are both equal to the tangent–chord angle, so VD = VE (= VF). Triangle VDE is therefore isosceles. H lies on j and J lies on g with HJ ∥ DE, so triangles VDE and VHJ are similar. This gives EJ/VE = DH/VD, and since VE = VD, EJ = DH. Next, e ⟂ j at D, and K lies on e. Also P_yH ⟂ j because P_yH ∥ h1 ∥ e, and KP_y ∥ j. So D, H, P_y, K form a rectangle, giving KP_y = DH. Hence EJ = DH = KP_y. The relation is EJ = VE·DH/VD with VE = VD, so EJ = DH = KP_y. As a check, in coordinates with j as the x-axis, KP_y = (v−d)(1+t²/v²) for any P_x = (v,t). The position of P_x is absorbed in the rectangle DHP_yK, and the numeric values agree: EJ ≈ KP_y ≈ 11.34.", "closes_goal": true}
